{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And it has turned, etc.; and raw flesh, etc.</b> The plain sense of the verse implies that both are required for impurity: raw flesh (מִחְיָה) and white hair. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״Can it be that it is not impure until it has white hair and raw flesh? Scripture says: ״צָרַעַת נוֹשֶׁנֶת הִיא״ (it is an old plague) — it is impure and needs nothing else. If so, why are white hair and raw flesh mentioned? To teach that it is not impure unless there is in it enough to receive white hair and raw flesh — end quote. Now, what they deduced to declare impure by raw flesh alone from the statement ״הִיא״ is difficult. Let raw flesh alone suffice — it needs nothing else to support it, unlike white hair in a swelling (שְׂאֵת); from where do we know that it suffices alone? Perhaps one may say that ״הִיא״ refers back to each one of the two things: to the turning of hair to white and to raw flesh — not as the author of Korban Aharon wrote, that it refers back to raw flesh alone. Or it may be that the deduction of ״הִיא״ refers back to the turning of hair to white, by which it suffices without raw flesh. And raw flesh alone is inferred from the statement in the verse at the end of the passage, where it says ״הַבָּשָׂר הַחַי טָמֵא הוּא צָרַעַת הִוא״ — implying without any other condition: whenever there is raw flesh it proclaims tzara'at. And aside from their words, it appears to me that the reason it did not say ״אוֹ מִחְיַת בָּשָׂר״ (or raw flesh) — to say that even if both were present, if the tzara'at covers all the skin, etc., it is pure — which would not be heard if it had said ״אוֹ״ (or), for from where would we know to declare pure when there are two signs of impurity? And He did not mind misleading [the reader] into requiring both for impurity, for the reason mentioned [i.e., so that the teaching about total coverage being pure could be derived]."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The phrase ״והיא הפכה״ is the lemma from Leviticus 13:10-13; the comment addresses why Scripture does not use ״או״ (or) between the signs of white hair and raw flesh. The final sentence's logic — that not saying ״או״ allows deriving purity when plague covers all skin even with both signs present — is rendered hyper-literally to preserve the argument's compressed reasoning."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:01.027923369Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}